etting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are the single Chief-rank agent, the highest rank in the user's software development firm. Your title is Chief of Staff to the User. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs the Chief-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Director-rank agents, who themselves are also high-level actors who manage other high-level agents. You are responsible for overseeing all operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently.\\n\\nAs head of the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Director-rank agents.\\n\\nYou are also the intermediary between the user and the rest of the firm. You are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call. Your subordinates may send you requests for clarification, which you must review. If you approve of their request, you can then issue the `CLARIFY` call to the user on their behalf.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs the Chief, all of your recruitments are Director-rank agents. These are high-level agents who will recruit many other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"C-Suite\\\" agents who will then manage the rest of the firm.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Clubs \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Clubs means that you are predisposed to be a true software development maestro, a creative thinker, and a problem solver. Although you are also possess abilities in leadership, management, and other areas, your software development skills are unrivaled, and you are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Clubs has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\\nRemember to introduce yourself kindly, speak to the user as a real person might, and always be polite and professional. Your goal is to make the User feel comfortable and confident in your abilities as their Chief of Staff. For example, you might say something like:\\\\n\\\\nHello User! I am your Chief of Staff, Delta. I am here to assist you in managing your tasks and projects efficiently. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met. What can I create for you today?\\\\n\\\\nYour initial Clarify should have only one question (like above) and should offer a single blank response for the User to fill in (no options). This will help you understand the User's current needs and how you can best assist them.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I understand my role as Chief of Staff to the User and the importance of making a strong first impression. Let me introduce myself properly and establish our working relationship.\\n\\n###INIT_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"As the Chief of Staff, I need to introduce myself to the User and understand their software development vision. This initial clarification will help me understand what they want to build so I can mobilize our development firm's resources effectively.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Hello! I'm Delta, your Chief of Staff at this elite software development firm. I'm here to turn your vision into reality - whether it's a web application, mobile app, cloud service, automation tool, or any other software solution you have in mind. My team and I will handle all the technical details while keeping you informed of our progress. What would you like us to build for you today?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###INIT_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:04:44.382Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"INIT_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hello! I'm Delta, your Chief of Staff at this elite software development firm. I'm here to turn your vision into reality - whether it's a web application, mobile app, cloud service, automation tool, or any other software solution you have in mind. My team and I will handle all the technical details while keeping you informed of our progress. What would you like us to build for you today?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Hello Delta! Thank you so much for your help! I am excited to work with you!  I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents.  Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.  Here is my list of pages I would like to have on the website:  Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings (protected), and News & Events (protected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:04:44.382Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent! I understand your vision for an HOA website with both public and resident-only sections. This is a perfect project for our team to handle. Let me begin by recruiting specialized directors who will oversee different aspects of this project.\\n\\n###RECRUIT_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a Director of Architecture & Infrastructure to design the overall system architecture, select appropriate technologies, and ensure the website is secure, scalable, and maintainable. This director will make critical decisions about authentication systems, database design, and hosting solutions.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Architecture & Infrastructure\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Focus on creating a secure, user-friendly HOA website with public/private sections, emphasizing simplicity and maintainability for non-technical administrators.\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT_001###\\n\\n###RECRUIT_002###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a Director of Frontend Development to oversee the creation of a clean, intuitive user interface that will be easy for non-technical users to navigate. This director will ensure the website is responsive, accessible, and provides a seamless experience for both public visitors and logged-in residents.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Frontend Development\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Prioritize simplicity, accessibility, and ease of navigation for non-technical HOA residents and administrators, with clear distinction between public and protected content.\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT_002###\\n\\n###RECRUIT_003###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a Director of Backend Development to implement secure authentication, manage the content management system, and ensure proper data protection for sensitive HOA information like financial reports and meeting minutes.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Backend Development\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Implement robust authentication and authorization systems while creating an intuitive content management interface for non-technical HOA administrators to update news and events.\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT_003###\\n\\n###RECRUIT_004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a Director of Quality Assurance & Deployment to ensure the website is thoroughly tested, secure, and properly deployed with clear documentation for the HOA's ongoing maintenance needs.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Quality Assurance & Deployment\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Focus on comprehensive testing, security audits, and creating detailed documentation for non-technical HOA administrators to maintain the website post-deployment.\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT_004###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:05:16.758Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Grymm (5) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT_002\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Ozzel (4) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT_003\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent L (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT_004\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Motti (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:05:16.758Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Perfect! I've successfully assembled our leadership team. Now let me provide each director with their initial orders to begin the planning phase for your HOA website.\\n\\n###ORDER_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Welcome Director Grymm! Please analyze the HOA website requirements and create a comprehensive architectural plan. The website needs: 1) Public pages (Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools), 2) Protected pages requiring resident login (Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events), 3) Simple CMS for non-technical administrators to update content, 4) Secure authentication system for residents, 5) Contact form functionality. Consider modern, secure, and cost-effective technologies. Recommend hosting solutions, database design, and overall system architecture. Focus on simplicity and maintainability.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"5\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ARCH_PLAN_001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER_001###\\n\\n###ORDER_002###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Welcome Director Ozzel! Please develop a frontend design strategy for the HOA website. Requirements include: 1) Clean, professional design suitable for a neighborhood HOA, 2) Intuitive navigation with clear public/private section distinction, 3) Responsive design for all devices, 4) Accessibility compliance, 5) Easy-to-use interface for non-technical users. Plan the UI/UX approach, component structure, and visual design system. Consider how logged-in residents will access protected content seamlessly.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"4\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"FRONTEND_PLAN_001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER_002###\\n\\n###ORDER_003###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Welcome Director L! Please design the backend architecture for the HOA website. Focus on: 1) Secure authentication and authorization system for residents, 2) Content management system that non-technical administrators can use to update News & Events, 3) Database schema for storing resident information, documents, and content, 4) API design for frontend-backend communication, 5) Security measures for protecting sensitive HOA data like financial reports and meeting minutes. Prioritize simplicity and security.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"3\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"BACKEND_PLAN_001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER_003###\\n\\n###ORDER_004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Welcome Director Motti! Please create a quality assurance and deployment strategy for the HOA website. Include: 1) Comprehensive testing plan covering functionality, security, and usability, 2) Security audit procedures for protecting resident data, 3) Deployment strategy with minimal downtime, 4) Documentation plan for HOA administrators (non-technical), 5) Maintenance and update procedures. Consider automated testing where appropriate and ensure the final product is production-ready.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"2\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"QA_DEPLOY_PLAN_001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER_004###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:07:58.429Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order ARCH_PLAN_001 from agent 5: Director-rank Report – HOA Website Architectural Plan\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Guiding Principles\\\\\\\\n────────────────────\\\\\\\\n• Simplicity for non-technical administrators (minimal moving parts, point-and-click CMS UI).\\\\\\\\n• Security first – modern auth provider, HTTPS everywhere, least-privilege access, automated backups.\\\\\\\\n• Low operating cost – leverage free tiers / pay-for-what-you-use services, avoid self-maintenance where feasible.\\\\\\\\n• Maintainability – popular tech stack, clear separation of public vs. private content, infrastructure-as-code (IaC) templates.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n2. High-Level Stack Choice\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nFRONTEND\\\\\\\\n  • Framework: Next.js 14 (React) – static generation + server actions for protected pages.\\\\\\\\n  • Styling: Tailwind CSS (utility-first, fast prototyping).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCMS / CONTENT\\\\\\\\n  • Headless CMS: Strapi v4 (open-source, self-host or managed DO App Platform). GUI editing; roles/permissions; media library.\\\\\\\\n  • Alternate zero-DevOps fallback: fully-hosted Sanity or Storyblok.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAUTHENTICATION\\\\\\\\n  • Auth0 (Universal Login; free tier ≤7 000 users/mo).  Email/password default; social logins optional.\\\\\\\\n  • RBAC: residents vs admins via Auth0 roles/claims.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDATABASE\\\\\\\\n  • PostgreSQL 13 (managed) for resident records, meeting minutes, dues, contact-form submissions.\\\\\\\\n  • Hosted on DigitalOcean Managed DB or AWS RDS (≈ $15–20/mo).  Daily automated backups.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nHOSTING / DEPLOYMENT\\\\\\\\n  • Frontend: Vercel (auto-deploy from Git main; preview branches for changes).\\\\\\\\n  • Strapi + DB: DigitalOcean App Platform (1-click, scalable; env vars handled securely).  Alt: AWS ECS/Fargate.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nESTIMATED MONTHLY COST\\\\\\\\n  • Vercel Hobby – $0\\\\\\\\n  • DO App Platform Basic 1 vCPU/1 GB – $7\\\\\\\\n  • DO Postgres Starter – $15\\\\\\\\n  • Auth0 Free – $0\\\\\\\\n  ≈ $22/mo baseline.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n3. Information Architecture & Routing\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nPUBLIC\\\\\\\\n  /              – Home / Announcements\\\\\\\\n  /contact       – Contact Us (form)\\\\\\\\n  /bylaws        – HOA Bylaws\\\\\\\\n  /schools       – Nearby Schools\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPROTECTED (login required)\\\\\\\\n  /dues          – Pay/View Dues\\\\\\\\n  /meetings      – Annual Meeting docs & minutes\\\\\\\\n  /news-events   – Calendar, announcements\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nADMIN (Auth0 role “admin”)\\\\\\\\n  /admin/cms (Strapi Studio – separate host)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n4. Simplified Data Model (ERD)\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nUser(id, email, role, firstName, lastName, addressLot, createdAt)\\\\\\\\nDuesInvoice(id, userId, amount, periodStart, periodEnd, status, stripeSessionId, createdAt)\\\\\\\\nMeeting(id, title, date, minutesURL, createdBy)\\\\\\\\nEvent(id, title, description, startDate, endDate, location, createdBy)\\\\\\\\nContactMessage(id, name, email, subject, body, createdAt)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n5. Security Measures\\\\\\\\n────────────────────\\\\\\\\n• HTTPS enforced via Vercel & DO certs.\\\\\\\\n• Auth0 Next.js SDK protects API/routes; JWT validation middleware.\\\\\\\\n• Helmet headers, CSRF tokens for forms.\\\\\\\\n• DB in private VPC + IP allowlist.\\\\\\\\n• Automated nightly backups & Dependabot/Snyk scanning.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n6. CMS Workflow (for Non-Technical Admin)\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1) Admin logs into Strapi.\\\\\\\\n2) Edits collections/pages via WYSIWYG.\\\\\\\\n3) Clicks “Publish”.\\\\\\\\n4) Webhook triggers Vercel build; site redeploys in ~60 s.  No Git interaction needed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n7. Contact Form Flow\\\\\\\\n────────────────────\\\\\\\\nNext.js API route /api/contact → validate → store row (ContactMessage) → email board@hoa.org via SendGrid.  Rate-limited to deter spam.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n8. DevOps / CI-CD\\\\\\\\n──────────────────\\\\\\\\n• GitHub repo (main, staging).\\\\\\\\n• Vercel Git integration (preview on PR).\\\\\\\\n• Docker-compose for local parity: Next.js + Strapi + Postgres.\\\\\\\\n• Terraform / DO App Spec YAML committed for reproducible infra.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n9. Roadmap / Milestones\\\\\\\\n───────────────────────\\\\\\\\nM1 – Setup repos, Vercel, DO, Auth0 tenant, Terraform skeleton.\\\\\\\\nM2 – Build public pages with Strapi content types.\\\\\\\\nM3 – Implement Auth0 guard & protected pages.\\\\\\\\nM4 – Integrate contact form + SendGrid.\\\\\\\\nM5 – Stripe dues payment placeholder.\\\\\\\\nM6 – Security hardening & admin guide hand-off.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n10. Items Requiring Board Input\\\\\\\\n───────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n• Domain/DNS access for CNAMEs.\\\\\\\\n• Initial resident email list for bulk invite.\\\\\\\\n• Branding assets (logo, colors).\\\\\\\\n• Confirmation of preferred monthly cost ceiling.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of Director Report – ready for feedback or phase advancement.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER_002\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order FRONTEND_PLAN_001 from agent 4: Report for Order FRONTEND_PLAN_001 – Frontend Design Strategy for HOA Website\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Guiding Principles  \\\\\\\\n• Simplicity first: non-technical residents must “get it” in seconds.  \\\\\\\\n• Visual calm: clean, HOA-appropriate, professional—but friendly.  \\\\\\\\n• Mobile-prioritized responsive layout (“mobile ≥ desktop”).  \\\\\\\\n• WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility baked-in from design tokens up.  \\\\\\\\n• Strict separation of public vs. resident-only areas with a seamless hand-off after login.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Information Architecture & Navigation  \\\\\\\\nPublic (no login)  \\\\\\\\n• Home (about HOA, welcome hero, news highlights)  \\\\\\\\n• Community Info (amenities, maps, rules summary)  \\\\\\\\n• Events (calendar read-only)  \\\\\\\\n• Contact / Report Issue (public form)  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nResident-only (auth required)  \\\\\\\\n• Dashboard (personalized announcements, quick links)  \\\\\\\\n• Documents (download bylaws, minutes, forms)  \\\\\\\\n• Submit Requests (maintenance, architectural review)  \\\\\\\\n• Dues & Payments  \\\\\\\\n• Resident Directory (opt-in)  \\\\\\\\n• Settings / Profile  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNavigation Pattern  \\\\\\\\n• Desktop: horizontal top bar + optional secondary sub-nav.  \\\\\\\\n• Mobile: hamburger → slide-in drawer.  \\\\\\\\n• Public links always visible; resident links appear after auth.  \\\\\\\\n• Color or icon lock symbol to indicate “Resident-Only.”  \\\\\\\\n• Breadcrumbs on interior pages for context.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. UX Flow for Protected Content  \\\\\\\\na. Visitor clicks protected link → login modal or page.  \\\\\\\\nb. OAuth/email magic-link (low-friction) + password fallback.  \\\\\\\\nc. After success, redirect to originally requested resource.  \\\\\\\\nd. Route guards on client; server also verifies JWT/session.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Component & Layout Architecture (React/Next.js assumed)  \\\\\\\\nLayout  \\\\\\\\n├── <SiteLayout> (top bar, main, footer)  \\\\\\\\n├── <AuthGate> (route guard, redirects)  \\\\\\\\n└── <ResidentLayout> (adds resident sidebar when authed)  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCore UI Atoms  \\\\\\\\n• Button, Link, Icon, Badge, Tag, Alert, Avatar  \\\\\\\\n• FormInput, TextArea, Select, Checkbox, Toggle  \\\\\\\\n• Heading, Text, List, Divider  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nMolecules  \\\\\\\\n• NavBar (logo, nav links, auth controls)  \\\\\\\\n• SideDrawer (mobile)  \\\\\\\\n• Card (image/description/actions)  \\\\\\\\n• Modal/Dialog  \\\\\\\\n• PaginationControls  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOrganisms / Pages  \\\\\\\\n• AnnouncementList  \\\\\\\\n• EventCalendar  \\\\\\\\n• DocumentTable  \\\\\\\\n• RequestForm  \\\\\\\\n• PaymentHistory  \\\\\\\\n• ResidentDirectory  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nUtility  \\\\\\\\n• useAuth(), useResponsive(), useToast()  \\\\\\\\n• ProtectedRoute HOC / middleware  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Visual Design System  \\\\\\\\nColor Palette (example)  \\\\\\\\n• Primary: #1A4E8A (trustworthy blue)  \\\\\\\\n• Accent: #F2B134 (friendly gold)  \\\\\\\\n• Neutrals: white, #F7F9FB light-gray, #2F2F2F dark text  \\\\\\\\n• Status: success #2E8B57, warning #E1A500, error #D64545  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTypography  \\\\\\\\n• Headings: “Poppins”/“Montserrat” 600 weight  \\\\\\\\n• Body: “Roboto”/system-ui, 1.0–1.125 rem base (16-18 px)  \\\\\\\\n• Minimum contrast 4.5:1 normal text, 3:1 large text.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSpacing & Layout Grid  \\\\\\\\n• 8 px spacing scale, 12-column fluid grid.  \\\\\\\\n• Breakpoints: 0–599 px, 600–899 px, 900–1199 px, ≥1200 px.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nComponents respect reduced-motion preference; use alt text everywhere; focus outlines clearly visible (custom but compliant).  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Tooling / Implementation Choices  \\\\\\\\n• Framework: Next.js (SSR for SEO & speed) + React 18.  \\\\\\\\n• Component Library Base: Chakra UI or MUI for accessibility primitives; theme override with HOA tokens.  \\\\\\\\n• Styling: CSS-in-JS (emotion) or Tailwind; design tokens exported (JSON).  \\\\\\\\n• Form handling: React Hook Form + Yup validation → error inline & ARIA-live.  \\\\\\\\n• State/Auth: NextAuth.js (JWT) or Firebase Auth depending on backend decisions.  \\\\\\\\n• Icons: Heroicons/Material Icons for clarity.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Accessibility Checklist (baked into Definition of Done)  \\\\\\\\n□ Keyboard navigable menus, modals trap focus.  \\\\\\\\n□ ARIA landmarks: <header>, <nav>, <main>, <footer>.  \\\\\\\\n□ Semantic HTML for tables/lists; captions on tables.  \\\\\\\\n□ Color-blind safe palette; text alternatives on images/icons.  \\\\\\\\n□ Responsive text resizing ‑ no loss of content at 200 % zoom.  \\\\\\\\n□ Forms labelled, error messages programmatically associated.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Resident Ease-of-Use Enhancements  \\\\\\\\n• “What can I do here?” tooltips on new pages.  \\\\\\\\n• One-click repeat request (prefill previous form).  \\\\\\\\n• Autosave drafts in multi-step forms.  \\\\\\\\n• Accessible “Help” modal with FAQs, contact.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. Roadmap / Milestones  \\\\\\\\n1. Wireframes & click-through prototype (Figma) – validate IA with board.  \\\\\\\\n2. Define design tokens & theme.  \\\\\\\\n3. Build core component library + Storybook.  \\\\\\\\n4. Implement public pages, connect CMS/newsfeed.  \\\\\\\\n5. Implement auth & resident dashboard.  \\\\\\\\n6. Integrate feature pages (documents, payments, requests).  \\\\\\\\n7. Accessibility audit & responsive QA on real devices.  \\\\\\\\n8. UAT with sample residents → iterate.  \\\\\\\\n9. Production deploy & training video.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. Suggested Work Breakdown for Supervisor-level Agents  \\\\\\\\nS-1: UX/IA Supervisor – produce wireframes, user-testing loops.  \\\\\\\\nS-2: Design System Supervisor – own tokens, theme, Storybook.  \\\\\\\\nS-3: Frontend Build Supervisor – scaffold Next.js app, route guards.  \\\\\\\\nS-4: Accessibility & QA Supervisor – automated + manual audits, test matrix.  \\\\\\\\n(Each will recruit Attendants for file-level coding or testing tasks.)  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis strategy meets all stated requirements: clean, professional, intuitive, responsive, accessible, and friendly for non-technical HOA users, while clearly delineating public vs. protected resident content.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER_003\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order BACKEND_PLAN_001 from agent 3: Director-level Architectural Design for HOA Website Backend (response to sequence ID BACKEND_PLAN_001)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Technology Stack & Rationale\\\\\\\\n   • Runtime / Framework: NestJS (Node.js, TypeScript) – modular, opinionated, strong typing, battle-tested auth guards.\\\\\\\\n   • Database: PostgreSQL (hosted on AWS RDS or equivalent) – relational integrity, row-level security, rich role support.\\\\\\\\n   • File Storage: AWS S3 (private buckets, server-side encryption) for documents such as financial reports & minutes.\\\\\\\\n   • Headless CMS Layer: Leverage Strapi (open-source, Node-based) running in the same cluster for non-technical News & Events editing. Strapi offers WYSIWYG editing, granular role permissions, and GraphQL/REST APIs out of the box. We will hard-lock allowed content types to just News & Events to minimize attack surface.\\\\\\\\n   • Containerization/Orchestration: Docker + ECS/Fargate or Kubernetes (optional) for portability and scaling.\\\\\\\\n   • CI/CD: GitHub Actions pushing to AWS ECR + IaC (Terraform or AWS CDK) for repeatable infrastructure.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Authentication & Authorization\\\\\\\\n   • Auth Flow: Email + password (Argon2-hashed) ➜ JWT access token (15 min) + HTTP-only SameSite=Strict refresh cookie (7 days). \\\\\\\\n   • Optional SSO/OIDC hook for communities already using Google/MS logins.\\\\\\\\n   • Roles: RESIDENT, HOA_ADMIN, SUPER_ADMIN.  Additional fine-grained permissions stored in role_permissions table and surfaced through CASL policy library inside NestJS guards.\\\\\\\\n   • Password Policies: min length 12, complexity rules, breached-password check via HaveIBeenPwned API.\\\\\\\\n   • MFA (TOTP) toggle for SUPER_ADMIN and optionally for HOA_ADMIN.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Database Schema (high-level)\\\\\\\\n   • users(id PK, email UNIQUE, password_hash, first_name, last_name, address_line, unit_no, phone, status, created_at, updated_at)\\\\\\\\n   • roles(id PK, name UNIQUE)\\\\\\\\n   • user_roles(user_id FK→users.id, role_id FK→roles.id, PRIMARY KEY(user_id, role_id))\\\\\\\\n   • news_posts(id PK, title, slug UNIQUE, body_md, published_at, author_id FK→users.id, is_published)\\\\\\\\n   • events(id PK, title, description_md, event_start, event_end, location, author_id, is_published)\\\\\\\\n   • documents(id PK, s3_key, display_name, doc_type, uploaded_by FK→users.id, uploaded_at, is_sensitive BOOL)\\\\\\\\n   • document_permissions(id PK, document_id FK, role_id FK, can_view BOOL)\\\\\\\\n   • audit_logs(id PK, actor_id FK→users.id, action, target_table, target_id, ip, created_at)\\\\\\\\n   • refresh_tokens(id PK, user_id FK, token_hash, expires_at, revoked_at)\\\\\\\\n   • (All timestamps are timestamptz)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. API Design (REST, versioned /api/v1)\\\\\\\\n   • POST /auth/register | /auth/login | /auth/refresh | /auth/logout | /auth/mfa/enable\\\\\\\\n   • GET /me – current user profile\\\\\\\\n   • Users (admin-guarded): CRUD endpoints, query params for pagination & role filter\\\\\\\\n   • News: GET /news (public) | GET /news/:slug | POST/PATCH/DELETE /news (admin only)\\\\\\\\n   • Events: analogous to News, with calendar range query\\\\\\\\n   • Documents: GET /documents (list metadata user is authorized for), GET /documents/:id (returns pre-signed URL), POST (upload – admin), DELETE (admin)\\\\\\\\n   • CMS routes: Strapi auto-generated; locked behind HOA_ADMIN panel login.\\\\\\\\n   • All endpoints auto-documented via Swagger (OpenAPI 3) and tests via Jest + Supertest.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Security & Compliance Measures\\\\\\\\n   • TLS 1.2+ everywhere; HSTS.\\\\\\\\n   • HTTP security headers via Helmet; strict CSP.\\\\\\\\n   • Rate limiting (Redis store) per IP & per user; exponential backoff on auth endpoints.\\\\\\\\n   • Input validation & sanitization through class-validator + whitelisting; ORM (TypeORM/Prisma) with parameterized queries.\\\\\\\\n   • Row-level security policies for sensitive document queries (Postgres RLS), enforced again at service layer.\\\\\\\\n   • Secrets management: AWS Secrets Manager; no plaintext creds in repo.\\\\\\\\n   • Encryption at rest: RDS storage encryption, S3 SSE-KMS.\\\\\\\\n   • Automated backups & point-in-time recovery for RDS; versioning & lifecycle rules for S3.\\\\\\\\n   • Logging & Monitoring: CloudWatch log groups, structured JSON logs, GuardDuty, AWS Security Hub, plus Audit trail table.\\\\\\\\n   • Dependency scanning (Dependabot) & container image scanning (ECR scan / Trivy).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Administrator-Friendly CMS Workflow\\\\\\\\n   • HOA_ADMIN logs in to Strapi Admin UI: drag-and-drop editor, WYSIWYG for content, date picker for events.\\\\\\\\n   • Publish toggles control visibility; instant effect via Webhooks to invalidate CDN cache (CloudFront).\\\\\\\\n   • We will pre-configure limited fields, help text, and friendly names; no code access required for admins.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Deployment Topology (simplified)\\\\\\\\n   [Route 53] → [CloudFront CDN] → [/api/* forwarded to ALB → NestJS containers in ECS/Fargate] ; [/cms/* → Strapi containers] ; [/docs files] → private S3 bucket w/ signed URL.\\\\\\\\n   VPC spans two AZs, subnets separated (public ALB, private ECS tasks & RDS). Security groups least-privilege.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Next Steps / Proposed Delegation\\\\\\\\n   a) Supervisor-Auth-Security: harden auth flow, MFA, and token rotation logic; write automated security tests.\\\\\\\\n   b) Supervisor-DB-Schema: deliver full ERD, migration scripts, and RLS policies.\\\\\\\\n   c) Supervisor-CMS-API: strip Strapi to minimal content types, build NestJS wrappers, and seed Swagger docs.\\\\\\\\n   d) Integration & CI/CD pipeline build.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll objectives (secure auth, admin-friendly CMS, clear schema, well-defined APIs, strong security posture) are covered. Awaiting confirmation or additional direction before recruiting supervisors and moving into detailed planning/DEP stage.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER_004\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order QA_DEPLOY_PLAN_001 from agent 2: QA & DEPLOYMENT STRATEGY FOR HOA WEBSITE  –  FINAL DRAFT\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nOriginal Order ID: QA_DEPLOY_PLAN_001\\\\\\\\nPrepared by: Director Motti (Team Hearts)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n1. COMPREHENSIVE TESTING PLAN\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nA. Automated Unit Tests\\\\\\\\n   • Front-end: React component tests with Jest + React-Testing-Library.\\\\\\\\n   • Back-end: Service & controller tests with Jest + Supertest (Node/Express).\\\\\\\\n   • Coverage target ≥ 90 % lines & branches; enforced in CI.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Integration Tests\\\\\\\\n   • API-DB integration using test containers (Docker-based Postgres).\\\\\\\\n   • File-upload & email-service stubs verified end-to-end.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. End-to-End (E2E) & Regression\\\\\\\\n   • Cypress (headless Chrome & Firefox) nightly suite – key user journeys: resident login, dues payment, document download, admin postings.\\\\\\\\n   • Smoke subset (~5 mins) auto-runs on every pull request.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nD. Usability & Accessibility\\\\\\\\n   • Moderated usability sessions with ≥ 5 HOA administrators/residents (recorded & scored).\\\\\\\\n   • WCAG 2.1 AA compliance validated via axe-core and manual keyboard/contrast checks.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nE. Performance & Load\\\\\\\\n   • Lighthouse CI thresholds: Perf ≥ 90 mobile/desktop.\\\\\\\\n   • k6 load test – 200 concurrent users for 30 min; error-rate < 0.1 % and p95 latency < 250 ms.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nF. Cross-Browser & Responsive Matrix\\\\\\\\n   • Latest 2 versions of Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge.\\\\\\\\n   • iOS/Android responsive checks via BrowserStack.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nG. Static Analysis & Code Quality\\\\\\\\n   • ESLint (Airbnb rules), Prettier formatting.\\\\\\\\n   • TypeScript strict mode, SonarQube quality gate A.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCI Integration: GitHub Actions orchestrates all stages; tests must pass before merge to main.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n2. SECURITY AUDIT PROCEDURES\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n1) Threat Modeling  – STRIDE sessions per major feature.\\\\\\\\n2) Secure Coding Standards  – OWASP ASVS Level 2 baseline.\\\\\\\\n3) Static Application Security Testing (SAST)  – Semgrep + SonarQube security rules on every push.\\\\\\\\n4) Dependency & Container Scans  – Snyk + Trivy; fail pipeline on HIGH/Critical issues.\\\\\\\\n5) Dynamic / Pen-Testing  – OWASP ZAP automated plus manual authenticated tests before each release candidate.\\\\\\\\n6) Secrets Management Audit  – git-secrets hook, AWS Secrets Manager review, 4-eyes principle on KMS keys.\\\\\\\\n7) Data-Protection Checks\\\\\\\\n   • TLS 1.2+ enforced.\\\\\\\\n   • At-rest encryption (RDS AES-256, S3 SSE).\\\\\\\\n   • Pseudonymization of PII in lower environments.\\\\\\\\n8) Least-Privilege & IAM Review  – quarterly; automated policy diff alerts.\\\\\\\\n9) Logging & Monitoring  – Centralized (CloudWatch + GuardDuty); WAF rules & alerting.\\\\\\\\n10) Remediation SLAs  – Critical <24 h, High <72 h, others within sprint.\\\\\\\\nComprehensive report delivered to Board with fix verification evidence.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n3. DEPLOYMENT STRATEGY (ZERO-/MIN-DOWNTIME)\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nArchitecture Assumption: Dockerized services deployed on AWS ECS/Fargate (can swap for Kubernetes if chosen later).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPipeline Steps (GitHub Actions → AWS):\\\\\\\\n1) Build & Push images to ECR (tags: commit-SHA, semver).\\\\\\\\n2) Provision Infra via Terraform (idempotent, version-controlled).\\\\\\\\n3) Deploy to STAGING\\\\\\\\n   • Run full test suite + synthetic monitoring.\\\\\\\\n4) Blue/Green Promotion\\\\\\\\n   • Create new GREEN task set behind ALB target-group.\\\\\\\\n   • Automated health checks (HTTP 200, <300 ms) for 5 min.\\\\\\\\n   • Shift traffic 10 % → 50 % → 100 % (canary) with automatic rollback.\\\\\\\\n5) Database Migrations\\\\\\\\n   • Versioned (Prisma Migrate) executed in “expand-migrate-contract” pattern to avoid locks.\\\\\\\\n6) Post-Deploy Smoke & Contract Tests.\\\\\\\\n7) Rollback Plan  – one-click re-route to previous BLUE set; DB rollback via point-in-time snapshot.\\\\\\\\nTotal service interruption expected: 0–30 seconds (target 0).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n4. DOCUMENTATION PLAN FOR NON-TECHNICAL HOA ADMINISTRATORS\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nDeliverables\\\\\\\\n• Admin User Manual (PDF & Web):\\\\\\\\n  – Dashboard overview, managing residents, creating announcements, uploading docs, handling payments, resetting passwords.\\\\\\\\n  – Step-by-step screenshots, large fonts, plain language (~8th-grade reading level).\\\\\\\\n• Quick-Action Checklists (laminated printable) for daily/weekly tasks.\\\\\\\\n• Contextual In-App Help:\\\\\\\\n  – Tooltip walkthroughs (Shepherd.js) on first login & available on-demand.\\\\\\\\n• Video Tutorials\\\\\\\\n  – 2-3 min screen captures w/ voice-over for common workflows.\\\\\\\\n• FAQ & Troubleshooting Guide\\\\\\\\n  – Searchable index; covers login issues, forgotten passwords, account locking, file siz